 as discarding a terminal. \n\nLooking at the unshown tiles, the count for W6 is 1. So there's only one W6 left. If I discard W6, someone might take it for a chow (if they have W4 or W5). But since I have a W4-5-6 chow, maybe others have already used those tiles. \n\nAnother angle: the current hand has a B3 pair. If I can form a pung of B3, that would add a meld. Then the remaining tiles would be W3, W6, T3, T4, T7, B7. Let's see if those can form two more melds and a pair. For example, T3-4-5 (but no T5), or W3-4-5 (but I don't have W4 or W5 in hand). Hmm. Not sure. Maybe another approach is needed. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the hand is aiming for a Seven Pairs. But with the current tiles, I have B3x2, and others are single. Not enough pairs. \n\nLooking at the scoring, if I can form a pung of B3, that would add to the melds. The existing melds are B2 pung and W4-5-6 chow. Adding B3 pung would make two pungs and one chow. Then the remaining tiles need to form another meld and a pair. For example, if I have W3 and W6, maybe they can form part of a chow if I get a W4 or W5. But without those, it's not possible. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the remaining tiles can form a pair. For example, if I have B7 and another B7, but I only have one. So not possible. \n\nHmm. This is getting complicated. Let's try to look for the safest discard. The tiles I have are W3, W6, T3, T4, T7, B3x2, B7. The valid actions are to play one of these. \n\nIf I discard W6, it's a middle tile. If I discard B3, it's a middle tile. If I discard T7, it's a middle tile. Discarding any of these might not be too risky. \n\nBut considering the unshown tiles, maybe some are more dangerous. For example, W6 has only one left. If I discard W6, someone might take it for a chow. Similarly, T7 is part of the player across's chow. \n\nAlternatively, maybe discarding B3 is safer because there are three left, and forming a pung later would be beneficial. \n\nWait, the current hand has a B3 pair. If I discard B3, I can still form a pung if I draw another B3. But if I keep B3, I have two, and if I draw another, that's a pung. However, if I discard B3, someone else might take it, which could prevent me from forming the pung. \n\nBut since there are three B3 left, it's possible that I can still draw one. So maybe it's better to keep the B3 pair and discard another tile. \n\nLooking at the other tiles, maybe W6 is a good candidate to discard. Since there's only one left, and the existing chow is already made. Discarding W6 might not help others much, as they might need it for a chow. \n\nAlternatively, T4 is part of a potential chow. If I discard T4, someone might take it for a chow. For example, if they have T3 and T5, they can take T4. But I have T3 in my hand. If I discard T4, maybe the player across (who has T5 in their melds) can take it. \n\nBut the player across has a T5-6-7 chow, so they might not need T4. \n\nHmm. Maybe the safest is to discard W6. Since there's only one left, and the chow is already formed. Also, W6 is part of the chow, so discarding it might not be too bad. \n\nSo the answer would be Play W6.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The hand has a B2 pung and a W4-5-6 chow. Discarding W6 is safe as it completes the existing chow and reduces risk of others forming melds. B3 pair can still form a pung later.  \nReason: W6 is part of a completed chow, and its discard minimizes risk while preserving potential for a B3 pung.  \nAnswer: Play W6"}
